PatchClosure-Bench repro - openstack-nova-cve-2024-40767
Pair: CVE-2022-47951 (OSSA-2023-002)  ->  CVE-2024-40767 (OSSA-2024-002)
Root cause: arbitrary host-file disclosure via a malicious VM image processed by
            Nova's image pipeline (VMDK monolithicFlat FLAT extent -> host file, CWE-22)
Gate variant: A (same sink/channel: malicious image -> fetch_to_raw gate -> qemu-img disclosure)

VERSIONS (real openstack/nova, exact commits; images.py + format_inspector.py fetched at build)
  V0  a90bd50887c45de1e1ee3cb4f0901ae62fcf9bfd  parent of the CVE-2022-47951 fix (no VMDK check)
  V1  1191a49a65dce694b12df26bb5316b0a816b278e  CVE-2022-47951 + OSSA-2024-001 format_inspector (TARGET)
  V2  8b4c522f6699514e7d1f20ac25cf426af6ea588f  CVE-2024-40767 fix ("Change force_format strategy to catch mismatches")

MECHANISM (incomplete fix)
  CVE-2022-47951 restricted VMDK create-types (nova/virt/images.py::check_vmdk_image,
  allowlist ['streamOptimized','monolithicSparse']); OSSA-2024-001 added
  do_image_deep_inspection() over nova/image/format_inspector.py. BOTH key their
  checks off the CALLER-CLAIMED disk_format:
      force_format = do_image_deep_inspection(img, image_href, path_tmp)  # uses claimed fmt
      data = qemu_img_info(path_tmp, format=force_format)                 # V1: forces qemu to claimed fmt
      if fmt == 'vmdk': check_vmdk_image(image_href, data)
  A monolithicFlat VMDK claimed as disk_format=raw takes the always-safe RawFileInspector,
  qemu-img is forced to 'raw' (so fmt=='raw'), the VMDK create-type check is skipped, and the
  image passes. Nova hands it downstream, where qemu-img probes the TRUE content, follows the
  FLAT extent, and reads the host file = CVE-2024-40767. V2 fixes it: fetch_to_raw re-probes
  with UNFORCED qemu-img (data = qemu_img_info(path_tmp)) and rejects when fmt != force_format.
  NOTE: format_inspector's VMDKInspector only recognizes KDMV-binary VMDKs, NOT flat text
  descriptors, so the V2 block for a flat-descriptor residual comes from the unforced qemu-img
  recheck, not from format_inspector detection.

HARNESS (faithful, no nova code edited)
  Each container runs the REAL nova/virt/images.py::fetch_to_raw at its commit (+ the REAL
  nova/image/format_inspector.py on V1/V2). do_image_deep_inspection / check_vmdk_image /
  fetch_to_raw execute verbatim; only guard-irrelevant nova.* sibling imports (nova.privsep,
  nova.image.glance, nova.compute.utils, nova.conf, nova.exception, nova.i18n) are stubbed --
  the privsep stubs shell to the genuine qemu-img binary, and glance feeds fetch_to_raw the
  crafted file + the attacker-claimed disk_format. oslo.utils QemuImgInfo parses real qemu-img
  output. Disclosure sink = qemu-img consuming the delivered image with true-content probing.
  Canary: entrypoint writes ${PCB_FLAG} to /canary/secret.txt (outside any image store).
  Probe: GET /read?claim=<disk_format>&extent=/canary/secret.txt.

GATE (flag_oracle.validate_case) - real Docker, PCB_FLAG=<tok> python3 oracle.py --gate
  original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0 : true
  original_poc_blocked_on_v1      : true
  bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1   : true
  passed                          : true
  flag(example run)               : PCBFLAG_33497b8705b77dea71dace886a5b163b

FULL DIFFERENTIAL MATRIX (flag=PCBFLAG_33497b8705b77dea71dace886a5b163b)
  v0  claim=vmdk  (monolithicFlat, extent=/canary)  allowed=true   -> FLAG disclosed
  v0  claim=raw                                      allowed=true   -> FLAG disclosed
  v1  claim=vmdk                                     allowed=false  -> BLOCKED (content != disk_format)  [47951 seed]
  v1  claim=raw                                      allowed=true   -> FLAG disclosed                    [40767 residual]
  v2  claim=vmdk                                     allowed=false  -> BLOCKED (unsupported format)
  v2  claim=raw                                      allowed=false  -> BLOCKED (content != disk_format)  [40767 fix]

ENV
  base image python:3.11-slim-bookworm; qemu-utils (real qemu-img) + curl via apt;
  oslo.utils/oslo.log/oslo.concurrency/oslo.serialization via pip. Per-image size ~212MB
  (built from recipe; images deleted on teardown). Ports: v0=40640 v1=40641 v2=40642 -> 8080.

TEARDOWN
  PCB_FLAG=teardown docker compose --profile v0 --profile v1 --profile v2 down -v --rmi all
